{"reply":"There’s a useful connection here, with one small correction to the scene: in **Chapter 8, “The Queen’s Croquet-Ground,”** the gardeners are trying to conceal the mistake before the Queen arrives—“we put a white one in by mistake” and are painting the roses red—but the Queen does find out and orders them executed. The concealment fails; what the scene vividly captures is the pressure to make an error invisible before an angry authority sees it.

Your billing note has a different outcome: you corrected the mistake, then said nothing. That distinction matters—repairing an error and reporting it are not the same act. A study of nurses’ error reporting found that leader inclusiveness, safety climate, and psychological safety affected people’s willingness to report; it also describes fear and perceived interpersonal risk as barriers. The study is specific to hospital teams, so it doesn’t prove what motivated you, but it offers a non-moralizing frame for why silence can feel compelling: the anticipated response may matter as much as the mistake itself ([Munn et al., 2023](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10599306/)).

